<<^ code="&#x0002;">>

<<~ ahu #/ruling>>

! The ruling — the arms name no hands

''Operator, 2026-09-12'' (recorded at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/scale-stories-basket-one">> #/the-shared-bag, quote license): "any nameless thing on the DreamNet — infra, persona, or cabal — may publish a Handle, a public Face/Heraldry". Beside it, two clauses the same paragraph carries: a Face ''may be worn in turn'' — "the KEL's rotate carries the mantle to the next holder, the co-sign threshold keeps a single turned hand from stealing it" — and a Face ''must never become'' "a registry of who wears what — the heraldry says //this is the ford-keepers' arms//, never //these are the ford-keepers//".

''The three sites naming that floor read as LICENCES, and no weld stands owed on any of them.'' The operator's words ride here ''verbatim'' — above, and again at #/the-herm-card where the great-grandchild's sentence gets quoted, and once as an edge at #/edges. This carrier therefore holds the ruling whole; the `loulou` marks ''where the operator said it'', which reads as provenance and never as a dependency. Re-aiming a provenance mark at a carrier that never carried the saying rewrites the citation and leaves the quote standing over a hole — silently, because the sentence reads on unchanged. ''A hand folding `scale-stories-basket-one` MUST NOT re-point these three.'' Should that floor end, these quotes stand as the record.

''What the ruling asks of the code.'' Three things publish a Face today and one thing cannot. `publishHandleFromDaemonDoc` reads the persona-KEL prefix off the daemon doc and REFUSES when it finds none (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/handle-publish.ts:33-41`, quote license): //"a face belongs to its persona, never itself; found the vessel first (fail-closed, the face never self-owns)"//. A Herm carries no persona-KEL prefix by law — `personaSlotCeiling("herm") === 0`, and the ceiling IGNORES its argument rather than clamping it (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/vessel-standing.ts:69-70`), because raising it "would put a human's key on a crossroads, the one thing the class exists to prevent" (`vessel-standing.ts:23-25`, quote license). So the one class the ruling most plainly names — infra — meets a refusal written as law.

''The reading that clears it, and it turns on WHOSE key.'' The refusal's own stated reason reads //"self-ownership resolves to no persona head and forecloses recovery"//, and both halves belong to a PERSONA's face. A Herm's owner is neither a persona nor the handle key itself: it is the Herm's ''PLACE DID'' — "a somewhere, not a someone … and never an operator DID" (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/identity-classes">> #/the-identity-class-ladder, quote license), the device-minted vessel key the keel already boots on. And its recovery never wanted a persona head: a Herm "re-establishes from its operator … The contract IS the whole authority relation; re-issuing it IS the recovery" (`identity-classes.mem` #/herm-establishment, quote license). ''The wall holds where it was built and does not reach here'': the refusal bars a face with NO owner, and a Place DID is an owner.

''And the primitive is already general.'' `mintHandleInceptionSet` takes `ownerSetMembers: readonly string[]` and checks only arity and threshold — no persona-prefix shape, no registry (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/handle-kel.ts:192-220`); the presentation check runs against an ''injected'' `OwnerHeadResolver` (`handle-kel.ts:614`), and the module "stays persona-KEL-DECOUPLED … nothing here imports the persona grammar" (`handle-kel.ts:37-38`, quote license). ''Heraldry for the nameless asks the handle-KEL for nothing new.'' What it asks for lives one layer up: a publish door that seats a non-persona owner, and a carriage relation that lets a faceless carrier hold a realm's PUBLIC registrations.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-three-nameless>>

!! The three nameless things — what each already holds

The ruling names three, and they differ by ''what holds the presenting key'', never by tier.

<<~ has Nameless Cabal "is/a collective with NO name of its own holds/its realm's charter (the genesis seal epoch IS the realm's name — realm-bag.ts:80-86) presents/the keptBy set of the books it keeps owner-set/the stewards' persona prefixes, k-of-n publishes/arms for the ford, never a roster of its keepers">>
<<~ has Nameless Infra "is/a Herm or an unlit hearth — a PLACE holds/a device-minted vessel key + a carriage contract, NO persona root (vessel-standing.ts:69-70) presents/its own vessel key owner-set/that one key, 1-of-1 publishes/where it stands and what it serves, never who passes">>
<<~ has Nameless Veiled-persona "is/a human face behind the dyad veil holds/a persona-KEL prefix at a handle index presents/the veiled key derived at that index owner-set/the persona prefix, 1-of-1 — the degenerate case that IS the personal face publishes/a glamour, already live (`lares handle publish`)">>

!!! ① A cabal — the arms of a thing with no name

''A cabal holds no name of its own'' and is keyed by its realm's doc id (memory `project_cabal_realm_nexus_planes.md`; enacted at `packages/lararium-mesh/src/realm-bag.ts:80-86` — `realmIdOfCharter` reads the GENESIS epoch cid, "fixed across every rotation — the realm's NAME"). So a cabal's Face names the realm it keeps, and its natural owner-set reads straight off the book: `keptBy` — "the stewards' persona-root nyms — the write set. Every one of them signs" (`realm-bag.ts:105-106`, quote license), already n-of-n and already charter-aware (`charterOfSteward`, `realm-bag.ts:131-133`).

''The threshold the ruling wants already rides in the record.'' A realm-bag registration counts only when every named steward signed (`realmBagRegistrationCounts`, `realm-bag.ts:221-249`); a Handle grafts only at the prior set's threshold (`handle-kel.ts:384`+). The two thresholds answer different questions — who KEEPS the book, who may TURN the name — and a cabal Handle whose owner-set equals `keptBy` fuses them by accident. ''They stay separate'' (#/questions Q1).

!!! ② An infra vessel — a Herm, or a hearth nobody has lit

''One floor, not two kinds of Herm'' (`vessel-standing.ts:96-120`): a crossroads nobody raised and a hearth whose archive holds shut stand in the SAME state, and `standAs(asked, archiveOpens)` returns `"herm"` for either (`vessel-standing.ts:126-129`). So the infra Face covers both, and it covers a hearth ''temporarily'' — the card's monotone `version` + `expiry` lease (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/handle-card.ts:65-71`) already carries a face that stops being fed.

''What it may honestly say.'' Where it stands (a shore URL), what it serves (the bulb by cid, `/bulb/manifest` · `/bulb/<cid>.bin` — `packages/lararium-node/src/bulb-read-face.ts:5-8`), and which crossroads it keeps (the carriage relay's gate key, STABLE across restarts and already domain-separated off the vessel seed — `packages/lararium-node/src/carriage-relay.ts:37-41, 74-77`). ''What it may never say'': who dialed it, what crossed it, or what any of it meant.

!!! ③ A veiled persona — the case already standing

`lares handle publish "<glamour>"` mints and announces a card owned by the persona-KEL prefix (`packages/lares-cli/src/commands/handle.ts:23-34` → `packages/lararium-node/src/commands/handle.ts:47-82` → `handle-publish.ts:25-47`). The veil ruling keeps this the ONLY outward door — the atom never presents, the Circles stay private, the Face alone faces out (memory `project_veil_ruling_stage0.md`). Nothing here moves; it stands as the shape the other two borrow.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-mantle>>

!! The mantle — a True Name worn in turn

''The shape.'' A Handle whose glamour's OWNER-SET rotates: Roberts cedes to Westley, the name reads continuously valid, and no observer can tell which hand presents. Canon already rules it, in the field's own vocabulary (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/handle-card">> #/the-mu, quote license): //"whether ONE human or MANY hold the right to present it stays unknowable by design, exactly as a cabal refuses 'which member signed'"//; a many-presented open name reads as a ''multiple-use name'' (Luther Blissett) and a chartered group as a ''collective pseudonym'' (Wu Ming).

!!! What the KEL actually guarantees, and what it does not

<<~ has Move Rotation "authorized-by/ANY current member (handle-kel.ts:522-524) moves/the presenting KEY, never the set guarantees/a lost or seized key re-seats under the SAME nym costs/nothing about who may present">>
<<~ has Move Graft "authorized-by/a THRESHOLD of the PRIOR set, presenter + distinct co-signers (handle-card.mem #/the-chain) moves/the presenting SET — the mantle passes guarantees/no ONE turned hand carries the name away when the threshold exceeds 1 costs/a 1-of-1 grafts by one willing hand — by construction">>
<<~ has Move Burn "authorized-by/the seated key (self) or any current member (owner) moves/nothing — it ENDS guarantees/terminal: 'nothing rotates or grafts past it' (handle-card.mem #/the-chain) costs/the name cannot be resurrected, which IS the property">>

''The guarantee, stated exactly.'' The graft threshold bounds ''how many hands must consent for the mantle to move''. It bounds nothing about how many hands hold it: a set of five with threshold 1 turns on any one hand; a set of one with threshold 1 is a person. `verifyHandleKel` counts distinct prior-set authorizers against the prior threshold structurally; `verifyHandleKelFull` additionally proves each signature against the member's authoritative head through the injected resolver (`handle-card.mem` #/two-tiers, quote license). ''Tier-1 alone cannot tell a mantle from a person'' — and that reads as the design, not a gap.

!!! ★ The collision the operator pointed at — a 1-of-1 cabal Handle ★

''A cabal Handle with a one-member owner-set IS a person wearing a mantle's name.'' Canon states the identity plainly: //"1-of-1 is the degenerate case that IS the personal face"// (`handle-card.mem` #/the-mu, quote license). So a cabal that publishes arms under a 1-of-1 set publishes ''a person's face with a collective's glamour'', and every recogniser who reads the glamour as a collective reads a claim the chain never made.

''May it exist?'' ''YES, and the house MUST NOT check it.'' Three grounds, each already standing:

# ''The unknowability is load-bearing.'' The whole point of the quorum-Handle reads that "whether ONE human or MANY hold the right to present it stays unknowable by design" (`handle-card.mem` #/the-mu). A gate refusing 1-of-1-for-a-cabal would have to KNOW the Handle names a cabal — which means a compiled claim about what a Handle is, one scope up. That IS the rejected registry (memory `project_registry_filter_law.md`: a compiled-about registry at ANY scope, even one Nexus, REJECTS the fork).
# ''A cabal with one steward is a real thing.'' `realmBagRegistrationCounts` accepts a single-steward record (`realm-bag.ts:225` refuses only the EMPTY set), and the multitude-of-one rides canon everywhere else — the self-recovery inception arms a 1-of-1 "the multitude-of-one, NAMED" (`identity-classes.mem` #/the-two-forks, quote license). A ford one household keeps until the second arrives is the ordinary case, not a lie.
# ''The honest cure runs at the READER, never the writer.'' A recogniser keeps a LOCAL petname and a card "can certify itself perfectly and STILL be a stranger" (`handle-card.mem` #/self-certifying, quote license). A reader who cares how many hands present a name reads `ownerSetMembers` + `ownerSetThreshold` off the chain it already holds — they ride OUTSIDE the cid and travel with the card (`handle-kel.ts:70-72`). ''The count is legible to anyone who asks; it is simply never asserted by the glamour.''

''What the house SHOULD do instead of a check: say it once, in the card's own prose.'' A glamour makes no claim about its owner-set; a recogniser wanting a collective reads the set. That sentence belongs in `handle-card.mem` #/the-mu as a kept edge, and nowhere in a gate.

!!! The one thing a mantle needs that a 1-of-1 does not

''A mantle worn in turn needs the graft to STAND WITHOUT the departing hand.'' `handle-kel.ts:31-35` records the current build honestly (quote license): //"for THIS build a graft authorized by ONE current member suffices (DPR's real case — Roberts cedes to Westley by one willing hand). TRUE k-of-n graft governance (a guild requiring a THRESHOLD to consent) rides DECLARED, not built (a skipped red — `handle-kel.test.ts`)"//. So the ruling's second clause — //"the co-sign threshold keeps a single turned hand from stealing it"// — names a guarantee the mint does not yet enforce. ''That gap belongs to the mantle, not to heraldry'': #/plan step 5.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-herm-card>>

!! The Herm's card — a Face with no persona, and the carriage that seats it

!!! The card

''Signed by the Herm's OWN vessel key, device-minted.'' Not a persona root (the class forbids one, `vessel-standing.ts:69-70`), not the relay gate key (that one derives domain-separated for the transport and stays a transport identity — `carriage-relay.ts:60-77`; joining them would rebuild the link the veil model spends its design preventing, `carriage-relay.ts:62-65`). The owner-set is the ''Place DID'': `mintHandleInceptionSet(handleKeyDid, [placeDid], 1, recoverySetHash)` — the same call `mintHandleInception` already sugars for a 1-of-1 (`handle-kel.ts:228-237`).

''It incepts ARMED, like every prefix.'' `mintHandleInceptionSet` THROWS on an empty `recoverySetHash` (`handle-kel.ts:198-200`). A Herm's recovery leaf derives domain-separated off its own vessel seed — the idiom `resolveRelayGateSeed` already carries (`carriage-relay.ts:74-77`: HMAC-SHA256 under a domain tag, yielding exactly the 32 bytes an Ed25519 seed takes). Nothing leaves the device.

''What it carries:''

<<~ has Field nym "the handle-KEL prefix over (handleKey, genesisOwnerSetHash, recoverySetHash) — handle-card.ts:56-58">>
<<~ has Field glamour "a shore-name a traveller reads — 'the crossroads below the first wall'. Never a legal identity, never an operator's name">>
<<~ has Field chain "the self-contained KEL — a reader verifies the head key with no board (Tier-1, handle-card.mem #/two-tiers)">>
<<~ has Field standing "OPTIONAL content-address of a vouch thread (handle-card.ts:76-77). A Herm MAY leave it null — a crossroads that claims no reputation claims nothing false">>
<<~ has Field fleetProof "ALWAYS null. A fleet-proof is a persona root's signature over the nym (handle-card.ts:78-84); a Herm holds none, and 'a face that claims no fleet claims nothing false'">>
<<~ has Field expiry "the freshness lease read against the recogniser's LOCAL clock (handle-card.ts:69-71) — an unfed crossroads goes stale on its own, which is exactly right for a place">>

''What it carries NOT: any read cap.'' The type has no field for one (`handle-card.ts:55-87`), and the class law is structural rather than flagged — "a Herm reads as a cap-stack that never declares the wiki cap … blind by structure, never by a flag a later bug could flip" (`identity-classes.mem` #/the-four-classes, quote license). ''The type refuses the cap'' — that reads as the CONTROL, not a test.

!!! The carriage contract — distinct from a member's contract-in, on purpose

''Today a Nexus knows exactly one way to seat a vessel, and it runs through a persona root.'' `runNexusContract` requires an admit to carry the operator's own "accepts carriage" seal, and `resolveContractIn` obtains it two ways only — a supplied out-of-band token, or a self-sign from a HELD persona seed (`packages/lararium-node/src/commands/nexus-contract.ts:124-146`). Both walk `generateOrLoadPersonaGroupRoot` / `loadPersonaGroupRootSeed` (`nexus-contract.ts:132-136`). A Herm holds neither, so `resolveContractIn` refuses with the doctrine's own line: //"A Nexus never conscripts an operator into carriage"// (`nexus-contract.ts:138-142`, quote license). ''The refusal reads correct and reaches the wrong subject'': it guards against conscripting a PERSON, and a Herm is a place.

''So the relation a Herm enters reads as a CARRIAGE contract, and canon already named it.'' #/herm-establishment rules that an operator "establishes a Herm by signing a Nexus carriage contract from a node vessel" and that the Herm's authority "rides the wax-stamped carriage contract … a frozen causal edge attesting //this operator carriage-contracted this Herm under epoch N//, never a live persona in the Herm" (`identity-classes.mem`, quote license). ''The word carriage already means this; the code has only ever meant the member's contract-in by it'' (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/carriage-registry.ts:78-84` — `contractSig.signer` MUST equal the entry's `nym`).

<<~moves seat/one-kind-member-contract-in -> seat/two-kinds-MEMBER-and-CARRIER on/a-vessel-key-signed-carriage-contract if/the-signer-is-a-PLACE-and-never-a-person do/admit-as-CARRIER-never-into-members>>

''The shape, stated so the fold cannot blur it:''

# ''A distinct DOMAIN.'' `CARRIAGE_CARRIER_DOMAIN`, minted beside `CARRIAGE_ENTRY_DOMAIN` and `CARRIAGE_CONTRACT_DOMAIN` in the registry (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/domains.ts:96-97`). An HKDF/HMAC `info` IS the separation (memory `project_crypto_spine_and_safety_ledger.md`), so a carrier seal can never present as a member's.
# ''Signed by the VESSEL key, and the carrier is the place.'' The signer equals the Place DID the entry names, exactly as `verifyContractIn` requires the member's seal be the member's own (`carriage-registry.ts:139-147`). No persona root is read, loaded, or minted anywhere on the path.
# ''Counter-signed by the founder's kahu quorum'' — the same k-of-n over the same charter epoch the member entry already verifies (`verifyMembershipQuorum`, `carriage-registry.ts:117-136`). A carrier still enters by consent of the house, and by its OWN consent: both seals, as the doctrine requires.
# ''It NEVER enters `members{}`.'' `foldCarriageSet` returns the member nym set the enforcement shore unions with the kahu floor (`carriage-registry.ts:236-257`); a carrier folds into a ''separate'' set and `holdsCarriagePeer` stays false for it (`packages/lararium-node/src/nexus-carriage.ts:213-217`). That reads as the CONTROL, and it keeps the doctrine whole: the members board is "the maximum the system ever holds about a contracting operator" (`membership-doctrine.mem` #/the-operator-contract) — a place is not an operator and does not belong in it.
# ''Its grant is exactly PUBLIC-by-hash.'' Not CONTRACT, not a read cap. The tier chain already names the ceiling: PUBLIC = `{world}` (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/cap-tier">> #/the-chain), and a realm-bag registration already declares PUBLIC "for a book the Herm carries BY HASH (the pointer travels; the doc still crosses on the realm's own lane)" (`realm-bag.ts:107-109`, quote license).

!!! What a PUBLIC registration grants such a card — follow and serve, never read

''The shore already reads a realm's PUBLIC books; it simply never sees one.'' `publicCasShore`'s realm lane walks, for each realm the vessel serves, the pointers its PUBLIC-tier registrations name, and skips every CONTRACT book with a comment that states the law (`packages/lararium-node/src/bulb-read-face.ts:99` — //"a CONTRACT book rides the realm's own lane, never the shore"//). It is unit-proven both ways (`packages/lararium-node/tests/herm-follows-realm-public.test.ts:55-59`). ''The gap sits one step upstream'': `realmStanding()` folds the Herm's OWN realm doc replica (`open-node-vessel.ts:1999`, `realm-plane.ts` `standing()`), and a Herm today replicates no realm doc, because nothing federates one to it.

''Two legs carry that, and only the first is missing.''

<<~ has Leg Charter "the Herm holds the realm's NAME — `lares nexus seal import <roster>` is a file placement with a verdict and NO persona read (packages/lares-cli/src/commands/nexus-seal.ts:643-676), so a Herm may already hold a charter, and `realmPlane.refresh` stands the realm on first sight (open-node-vessel.ts:1660, realm-plane.ts refresh) ~ STANDS TODAY">>
<<~ has Leg Federation "A must federate the realm doc + each PUBLIC-registered book to the Herm's peer. `RealmBagGate.mayFederate` offers four doors (realm-bag.ts:481-494): the base shelf · `holdsCharterPeer` (a socket THIS vessel dialed to the charter's hearth) · the registration's own `keptBy`/`holdsCharter` by proven contract nym · `membership.holdsCarriagePeer`. A Herm proves NO contract nym (it presents no persona-root edge — nexus-carriage.ts:60-74) and stands in NO members set, so every door reads false ~ THE SEAM">>

''So the cure is one clause on the realm leg, and it is the narrowest one available.'' `RealmCharterConsult` gains a `carrierPeer(peerId)` question answered off the vessel's OWN carrier fold; `RealmBagGate.mayFederate` opens for such a peer ''only when the document's own registration declares PUBLIC'' — `rec.readTier === "public"`, read off `#byDocId` the gate already keeps (`realm-bag.ts:423, 489`). ''A CONTRACT-tier pointer still 404s through the Herm'' — that reads as the CONTROL, and it holds at TWO independent shores: the gate never federates the doc, and the shore would skip it even if it did (`bulb-read-face.ts:99`).

''And carry ⊥ read survives untouched.'' The Herm gains BYTES a pointer in a world-readable book names, which the world may already ask any hearth for. It gains no keyring, no `@cad` secret, no BeeKEM cap; the read-floor stays a causal-island-local FACT rather than a policy (`identity-classes.mem` #/the-mesh-breathes, quote license). ''The Herm holds the HINT, never the read-cap'' — the great-grandchild's sentence the operator already wrote (`scale-stories-basket-one.mem` #/the-shared-bag, moʻolelo clause 3).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/what-it-never-becomes>>

!! What heraldry must never become

<<~ has Never Roster "no surface anywhere accepts a COLLECTION of others' Handles. The handle-KEL already holds this line — 'the module handles ONE chain per call and exposes NO surface accepting a collection of others' Handles' (handle-kel.ts:41-43, quote license) — and the registry filter rules it at every scope (memory project_registry_filter_law.md: a compiled-about registry at ANY scope, even ONE Nexus, REJECTS the fork). A Herm publishing its own card passes; a Herm compiling the cards it relayed does not.">>
<<~ has Never Read-cap "the HandleCard type carries no field for one (handle-card.ts:55-87) and a carrier's grant stops at PUBLIC-by-hash (#/the-herm-card). A Face is a name presenting itself, never an authorization; 'a CAP answers what may I do, a STANDING answers what do I count as' and only the cap rides a device (membership-doctrine.mem #/the-carried-cap).">>
<<~ has Never Seat "a card confers no membership, no vote, no quorum share. A seat moves by the charter's own k-of-n (identity-classes.mem #/the-office: the quorum SEATS, and MUST NOT sit in the renewal path); a Face moves by its own publish. The cabal Handle names the ford's arms and seats nobody in the cabal — and a carrier contract seats no member (#/the-herm-card ③-④).">>
<<~ has Never Who-wears-it "the ruling's own words: the heraldry says //this is the ford-keepers' arms//, never //these are the ford-keepers//. `ownerSetMembers` travels on the chain for a reader who asks (handle-kel.ts:70-72) and is ASSERTED by nothing — no index, no board fold, no glamour claim (#/the-mantle).">>

''The one gate that would violate all four at once'': a check refusing a 1-of-1 owner-set "because a cabal must be many". It would need to know which Handles name cabals, which is a compiled about-registry; it would read the owner count as a claim the glamour never made; and it would turn a present-tense arity into a standing. ''Refused by design, not by omission.''

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/plan>>

!! The plan — red → cure → CONTROL

Ordered so each step's red stands alone and its CONTROL rides beside it. Spirit-hours name a tasked spirit's walk at this codebase's grain, never a human's.

!!! Step 1 · The carrier contract kind ~ 3 spirit-hours

* ''RED'' (`packages/lararium-mesh/tests/carriage-carrier.test.ts`, new): a carriage contract signed by a VESSEL key verifies as CARRIER; the same bytes presented as a member `contractSig` fail `verifyContractIn` (the domain separates them); `foldCarriageSet` over a board carrying only carrier entries returns the EMPTY member set.
* ''CURE'': `CARRIAGE_CARRIER_DOMAIN` in `domains.ts` beside `:96-97`; `carrierContractBytes` + `signCarrierContract` + `verifyCarrierContract` + `foldCarrierSet` in `carriage-registry.ts`, each mirroring the member shapes verbatim (`:96-114`, `:139-147`, `:236-257`) and holding no key.
* ''CONTROL'': the existing `carriage-registry` suite stays green byte-for-byte (a member admit still needs its persona-signed contract-in); a nonsense-domain seal verifies as neither.

!!! Step 2 · The founder's door accepts a carrier ~ 3 spirit-hours

* ''RED'' (`packages/lararium-node/tests/nexus-contract-carrier.test.ts`, new): `runNexusContract({ action: "carry", nym: <herm vessel key>, carrierSig })` lands an entry the carrier fold counts; `runNexusMembersList().members` does NOT contain it; a `--carrier` admit with NO seal REFUSES with a named error.
* ''CURE'': a `carry` action beside `admit`/`revoke`, resolving its seal through a `resolveCarrierIn` that reads a supplied token ONLY — never `generateOrLoadPersonaGroupRoot`, never `loadPersonaGroupRootSeed` (the whole point; `nexus-contract.ts:124-146` stays untouched for `admit`).
* ''CONTROL'': `personaSlotCeiling("herm") === 0` and `refuseSlot("herm", …) === "faceless-by-class"` stay green (`packages/lararium-mesh/tests/vessel-standing.test.ts`); `lares persona new 0` on a Herm still refuses (`packages/lares-cli/src/commands/persona.ts:88-91`).

!!! Step 3 · The realm leg opens for a carrier, at PUBLIC only ~ 4 spirit-hours

* ''RED'' (`packages/lararium-mesh/tests/realm-bag-carrier-leg.test.ts`, new): a peer the consult names a CARRIER federates a bag whose standing registration declares `readTier: "public"`; the SAME peer draws false for a `readTier: "contract"` bag AND for the realm doc itself.
* ''CURE'': `RealmCharterConsult` gains optional `carrierPeer?(peerId): boolean` (`realm-bag.ts:389-404`); `mayFederate` adds ONE clause after the charter doors and before the Nexus lane (`realm-bag.ts:481-494`), gated on `this.#byDocId.get(documentId)?.readTier === "public"`. `makeRealmCharterConsult` answers it off the carrier fold (`nexus-carriage.ts:246-268`).
* ''CONTROL'': every vector in `realm-bag-realm-leg.test.ts` stays green unchanged; a carrier peer with NO registration naming the doc draws the same false a stranger draws.

!!! Step 4 · The e2e flip ~ 4 spirit-hours

* ''RED'': ⑦ in `tests/e2e/herm-reshares-public-blob.e2e.test.ts` (today `test.fails`). `openStagedFleet` gains, after A founds: A seats a charter (`nexus rite cabal`), exports it, the Herm imports it, A carries the Herm by its vessel key, the Herm dials A's `/ws`, and A registers `bags/lares --tier public`.
* ''CURE'': the harness lines above (`tests/harness/instance.ts:311-349`) plus a `LAR_JOIN_SYNC` on the Herm's daemonEnv. No product change beyond steps 1–3.
* ''CONTROL'': the file's three standing CONTROLs hold unchanged — a ghost cid draws the byte-identical 404, the boot CAS still serves at `/bulb/<cid>.bin`, C's ghost fetch reads `held:false` and writes nothing. ''Plus one new'': a CONTRACT-tier registration's pointer draws 404 through the same Herm in the same run.

!!! Step 5 · The publish door seats a non-persona owner ~ 3 spirit-hours

* ''RED'' (`packages/lararium-mesh/tests/handle-publish-place.test.ts`, new): publishing with a Place-DID owner mints a card whose `fleetProof` reads null and whose chain verifies Tier-1 with no persona replica anywhere; publishing with NO owner at all still REFUSES with the line at `handle-publish.ts:37-41`.
* ''CURE'': `publishHandleFromDaemonDoc` takes an explicit owner rather than deriving one — the persona-KEL prefix when a daemon doc carries it, the vessel's Place DID when the vessel stands faceless. The refusal narrows from //no persona prefix// to //no owner//, which is what its own comment already argues.
* ''CONTROL'': `handle-publish.test.ts` stays green for the persona path byte-for-byte; a card minted for a Place carries `fleetProof: null` and a reader that demands a fleet-proof refuses it.

!!! Step 6 · The mantle's true threshold ~ 4 spirit-hours

* ''RED'': the skipped red `handle-kel.ts:31-35` already names (`handle-kel.test.ts`) — a graft over a k-of-n set authorized by ONE hand must FAIL when k > 1.
* ''CURE'': `mintHandleGraft` gathers `graftSigs` and `verifyHandleKel` counts distinct prior-set authorizers against the prior threshold, the shape `handle-card.mem` #/the-chain already documents.
* ''CONTROL'': a 1-of-1 graft by one willing hand still stands (Roberts cedes to Westley) — the degenerate case must not regress.

''Order note — REFUTED BY MEASUREMENT, 2026-09-13.'' Steps 1–4 were written to flip ⑦, and they do not. Two circles stand behind the one they close, and the e2e's own head carries both: the boards NEVER MEET (a crossroads board keys on the vessel's own verifying key, so the keeping hearth's announce lands where only it reads — measured as `0 PUBLIC book(s) on the board` on the Herm, with the fork between a hearth-keyed board and a realm-keyed one owed to the operator), and behind that the Herm holds no carriage CLIENT, so nothing ever makes it FETCH the bytes it would serve. The steps still stand and are still owed — they are necessary under every one of the three shapes — and the claim that they suffice does not. ''What the order note gets right'': steps 1–4 touch the Face not at all. Steps 5–6 stand the Face for the nameless and close the mantle's guarantee. ''They are separable'', and a session short on hours should take 1–4 whole rather than 1–6 partly.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/questions>>

!! Questions for the operator

# ''Q1 — does a cabal's Handle owner-set EQUAL `keptBy`, or ride beside it?'' Equal reads elegant and fuses two thresholds that answer different questions (who keeps the book ⊥ who may turn the name), so a steward leaving the book would move the arms. Beside reads honest and asks a cabal to mint a second set. ''The design leans BESIDE'', and names the lean rather than taking it.
# ''Q2 — may a Herm's card name its operator at all?'' #/herm-establishment says the authority relation rides the wax-sealed contract, "never a live persona in the Herm". A card carrying an `attest` of //"contracted under charter epoch N"// claims the relation without naming a human, and verifies reader-locally like every attestation (`handle-card.mem` #/the-chain). ''Lean: yes, as an attestation of the EPOCH, never of the operator.''
# ''Q3 — does an unlit hearth publish arms?'' `standAs` already returns `"herm"` for it (`vessel-standing.ts:126-129`), so the shape permits it; the cost reads that a hearth later lit would want to burn the place-card and publish a persona card, which the terminal burn makes clean. ''Lean: yes, and the burn is the right ending.''
# ''Q4 — a carrier contract's LEASE.'' A member entry is monotone-versioned and revocable by quorum (`carriage-registry.ts:236-257`). A carrier could instead expire by non-renewal — the shape `identity-classes.mem` #/the-office prefers ("the quorum SEATS; the renewal rides the lease-epoch roll"). ''Lean: non-renewal'', but it adds a pace reader this step does not otherwise need.
# ''Q5 — which glamour does a Herm show?'' A shore-name a traveller reads, or the empty string. A named crossroads is friendlier and is one more correlatable datum across restarts. ''Lean: operator-set, defaulting to empty.''

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/edges>>

!! Edges — what this design touches, and what it must not

''What it moves'': `domains.ts` (one constant) · `carriage-registry.ts` (a parallel carrier family) · `nexus-contract.ts` (a `carry` action, the `admit` path untouched) · `realm-bag.ts` (`RealmCharterConsult` + one `mayFederate` clause) · `nexus-carriage.ts` (the consult answers it) · `handle-publish.ts` (the owner becomes explicit) · `tests/harness/instance.ts` (`openStagedFleet` seats the Herm).

''What it MUST NOT move'': `personaSlotCeiling` and `refuseSlot` (`vessel-standing.ts:69-91`) — the ceiling stays 0 and stays argument-ignoring; `verifyContractIn` (`carriage-registry.ts:139-147`) — a member's seal stays the member's own; the CONTRACT lane of `publicCasShore` (`bulb-read-face.ts:99`); the `@daemon` plane, which never crosses (memory `project_face_join_and_plane_law.md`).

